    {
      "title": "Tutorials - Skills you Should Know",
      "slug": "tutorials-skills-you-should-know",
      "displayOrder": 21,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 291,
        "lessonOrder": 21
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\nThe front band of this pattern is designed to be knit in one LONG piece and attached by hand.  The pattern offers points to place markers corresponding with the sections of the body.<br>Depending on your yarn, stitch pattern(s) and gauge, you'll need to take time to complete this step carefully. <br><b>Options</b>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Knit a long stockinette band, with a needle out of work in the center for a crisp turning row</li>\r\n<li>Use the purl side for contrast if you like</li>\r\n<li>Knit a Full needle Rib band</li>\r\n<li>Pick up around the front edges and neckline with a hand knitting needle and knit your band in ribbing, garter stitch, seed stitch or stitch pattern of your choice</li>\r\n<li>Use sew-as-you go to attach the band as you knit it (practice on your swatch first).</li>\r\n<li>Add a small (or large) ruffled edge</li>\r\n<li>Work a decorative crochet edge</li>\r\n\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail zoom\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/42/5.jpg\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4687,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151857756",
              "title": "Shortrow Ruffle",
              "legacyComponentId": 4690,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Front edge finishing",
          "slug": "front-edge-finishing",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3353,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/42/hem.jpg\">\r\n\r\nBecause this is a sideways knit, the lower hem may need some attention.  <br><ul>\r\n<li>Depending on your stitch pattern, you may be able to just use the selvage edge of your knitting</li>\r\n<li>Work a crochet edge </li>\r\n<li>Knit a LONG band and attach it by hand, similar to the front edge</li>\r\n<li>Work a \"built-in\" I-cord edge as you knit the body of the sweater</li>\r\n<li>Create a sawtooth edge and attach to the edges</li>\r\n<li>Depending on your stitch pattern, create a hem as you knit.\r\nLeave a needle out of work along the hem edge as you knit. Fold along this needle out of work and stitch in place to create a hem facing.<br></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4688,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859733",
              "title": "Sawtooth Edges on the Machine",
              "legacySlot": 1,
              "legacyComponentId": 4689,
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
