          "title": "Hem finishing",
          "slug": "hem-finishing",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3361,
            "blockType": "Video"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n    No matter how you are going to finish the front edges of your sweater, for best results, you'll want the edge to be as smooth as possible. <br>My pattern Is telling me to cast on 54 (the A dimension), then increase 6 stitches 2 times, and increase 5 stitches 8 times. This will create stairsteps that will be difficult to join to a band or finish.<br><b>For a smooth front edge:</b><br><ol class=\"instruction-list\">\r\n            <li>Cast on the ENTIRE WIDTH (the \"E\" Dimension)</li>\r\n            <li>Put all needles except the \"A\" needles in hold</li>\r\n            <li>Substitute Short Row Increases for the increases indicated in the pattern</li>\r\n            <li>At the end of the sweater (the matching front) work Short Row Decreases for a smooth edge before binding off.</li>\r\n        </ol>\r\n        <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/42/legend.jpg\">\r\n    \r\n    \r\n        <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/42/stair_steps.jpg\"><br>\r\n        <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/42/v_neck_pattern.jpg\">\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/42/please_watch.gif\"><br>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 4676,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151860040",
              "title": "Short Row Increases and Decreases",
              "legacyComponentId": 4673,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Cast on the Front Edge",
          "slug": "cast-on-the-front-edge",
          "order": 3,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3363,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "When casting on stitches at the edge of your knitting, the best way to prevent headaches is to use a cast on rag.<br><i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> Use this for the front edge cast on and the cast on at the armhole openings.",
              "legacyComponentId": 4682,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151860044",
              "title": "Creating a Cast on Rag",
